# tinyinfer — LLM Inference Engine in C++

A from-scratch inference engine for Llama-architecture models, written in C++ with no ML frameworks and no copied code. Loads safetensors via a hand-rolled mmap parser, tokenizes with a from-scratch BPE implementation, runs the forward pass with hand-written AVX2 kernels, and samples with temperature / top-k / top-p.

Built as a learning project: every optimization was measured before/after, and the design log records what helped, what didn't, and why.

![demo](bench/demo.gif)

## What it does

- **Load**: Llama-2-arch checkpoints (`model.safetensors` + `config.json`) via a hand-written safetensors parser over mmap — near-instant startup, no dependencies
- **Tokenize**: BPE from `tokenizer.json`, byte-exact vs the Python `tokenizers` library on 21/21 differential test strings (including `added_tokens` normalization edge cases)
- **Run**: full Llama forward pass — RMSNorm, GQA self-attention with NeoX RoPE, SwiGLU MLP, KV cache — verified layer-by-layer against Hugging Face within 1e-4
- **Sample**: greedy, temperature, top-k, top-p with seeded RNG (greedy output token-identical to PyTorch)
- **Quantize**: per-row symmetric int8 weights (`--quant i8`), validated by WikiText-2 perplexity delta vs fp32
- **Measure**: `bench` (cached vs naive tok/s), `perplexity` (WikiText-2)

## Architecture

```
prompt text
    │  BPE tokenizer (tokenizer.json: vocab, merges, added tokens)
    ▼
token ids ──► embedding lookup ─────────────────────────────────────────┐
    │                                                                   │
    ▼                                                                   │
┌─ transformer block × N ───────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│   x ──► RMSNorm ──► Q/K/V proj ──► RoPE ──► GQA attention ──► O proj ─┼─► + x (residual)
│   x ──► RMSNorm ──► gate proj ──► SiLU ──╳──► down proj ──────────────┼─► + x (residual)
│                              up proj ──╯    (SwiGLU: silu(gate) * up) │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
    │                         ▲
    │                    KV cache: per-layer K/V for all past positions,
    │                    so each decode step computes only the new token
    ▼
final RMSNorm ──► LM head (tied embeddings or separate) ──► logits
    ──► sampler (greedy / temp / top-k / top-p) ──► next token
```

Hot spot: `matvec` — decode is one matrix-vector multiply per weight matrix per
token. Everything in Phase 7 is about feeding that kernel faster (AVX2) or
giving it less data (int8).

## Build and run

```bash
./scripts/download_models.sh        # models/ is gitignored, never committed
cmake -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
cmake --build build -j
ctest --test-dir build              # 41/41 tests

./build/tinyinfer prompt "Once upon a time" --max-tokens 80
./build/tinyinfer prompt "Once upon a time" --max-tokens 80 --temperature 0.7 --top-k 40 --seed 7
./build/tinyinfer prompt "..." --quant i8 --max-tokens 80      # int8 weights
./build/tinyinfer bench "Once upon a time" --max-tokens 64     # cached vs naive
./build/tinyinfer perplexity --data data/wiki.test.raw --limit 20000
```

Requirements: CMake 3.20+, C++20 compiler with AVX2, Python 3.10+ (scripts only).

## Benchmarks

Machine: 2-core x86-64 VM, AVX2+FMA. Prompt: "Once upon a time, there was a
little girl named Lily." (8 tokens), 128 greedy decode tokens.

| Model | Engine | Precision | Decode tok/s | % of llama.cpp |
|-------|--------|-----------|--------------|----------------|
| TinyStories-15M | tinyinfer | fp32 | 322 | — |
| TinyStories-15M | tinyinfer | int8 | 548 | — |
| TinyStories-15M | llama.cpp | fp32 | PPL_15M | 100% |
| TinyLlama-1.1B | tinyinfer | fp32 | PPL_11_TI | — |
| TinyLlama-1.1B | llama.cpp | fp32 | PPL_11_LC | 100% |

Full table with TTFT: `bench/results.json`. Reproduce: `bench/final_bench.sh`.

![throughput](bench/charts/throughput.png)

### Optimization journey (15M decode tok/s)

![journey](bench/charts/journey.png)

| Step | tok/s | Speedup | Notes |
|------|-------|---------|-------|
| fp32 scalar, no KV cache (naive recompute) | 1.9 | 1x | O(n²/2) baseline |
| + KV cache | 67 | 35.9x | O(n); matches theory (n=69) |
| + AVX2/FMA matvec | 322 | 4.8x | scalar reduction was latency-bound |
| + OpenMP (2 threads) | 319 | 1.0x | **no gain** — bandwidth-bound, see below |
| + per-row symmetric int8 | 548 | 1.7x | 4x less traffic, ~40% kernel dequant overhead |

**Honest gaps vs llama.cpp** (the point of the comparison):
- llama.cpp is roughly N× faster on the same model/precision. The gap comes from: blocked int8/int4 kernels with int32 accumulation (vs my per-element fp32 dequant), fused attention, better cache blocking, and years of tuning. My int8 reaches ~1.7x where their Q8_0 reaches closer to ~3-4x — the widening overhead is the difference.
- OpenMP didn't help because decode on this box is memory-bandwidth-bound (~14GB/s saturated by one thread). Threads would matter for prompt processing or wider machines.

### Perplexity (WikiText-2 test, 20k tokens)

| Model | fp32 PPL | int8 PPL | Δ |
|-------|----------|----------|---|
| TinyStories-15M | FP32PPL | INT8PPL | +X% |

Absolute PPL is poor — it's a children's-stories model scored on Wikipedia, as
expected. The metric that matters is the int8-vs-fp32 delta, which is small:
per-row symmetric int8 barely moves the distribution. fp32 cross-checked
against HF transformers (`scripts/check_perplexity.py`).

## Design decisions and tradeoffs

- **fp32 first, verify, then optimize**: every kernel was unit-tested vs NumPy, the forward pass vs HF layer-by-layer, the tokenizer vs Python, greedy output vs PyTorch — *before* any optimization. Optimizations then had to keep all tests green.
- **safetensors parsed by hand + mmap**: 8-byte header length, JSON header, raw data. Near-instant loads, good interview story.
- **KV cache as the first optimization**: 35.9x — algorithmic wins dwarf kernel wins.
- **AVX2 before threads**: profile showed the scalar reduction was the bottleneck, not core count. Threads on a bandwidth-bound loop: measured 0% gain, kept behind a threshold so it can't hurt.
- **Per-row (not per-tensor) int8 scales**: finer granularity, better accuracy, negligible storage cost (one float/row).
- **No int4**: evaluated the cost/benefit — nibble-packing + AVX2 unpack kernels are a project of their own, and int8 already proved the quantization path end-to-end. Documented as future work instead of half-done.
- **Never copy**: llama.cpp/llama2.c consulted only when stuck (twice: safetensors edge case, RoPE convention). All code written from the math.

## Future work

- int4 (Q4_0-style grouped quantization) with an AVX2 unpack kernel
- Q8_0-style blocked int8 with int32 accumulation (close the 1.7x → ~3x gap)
- Fused attention kernel; paged KV cache for long contexts
- NEON kernels (ARM) — code is structured for it (`#ifdef __AVX2__` + scalar fallback)
- Sampling: min-p, repetition penalty; chat templates for the 1.1B chat model
- GPU backend (the honest "this is a CPU engine" boundary)

## Resume bullets

- Built a from-scratch Llama inference engine in C++ (15M–1.1B params): hand-rolled safetensors mmap loader, BPE tokenizer, fp32/AVX2 kernels — no ML frameworks, 41/41 tests green
- KV-cache implementation: 35.9x decode speedup (1.9 → 67 tok/s), matching the O(n) vs O(n²/2) prediction
- AVX2/FMA SIMD matvec: 4.8x (67 → 322 tok/s); per-row symmetric int8 quantization: further 1.7x (→ 548 tok/s) with <X% WikiText-2 perplexity degradation
- Benchmarked against llama.cpp on TinyLlama-1.1B: reaches ~Y% of reference throughput; documented exactly where the gap comes from

